# Trees, Trees, and Trees, Oh My

@body

![Original comparison of equivalent rotations of the same four-species tree](assets/lect11b_restored/image_018.webp)

- [Rotate around a branch point: the relationships stay the same.]{color=#8D281E}
- [All four drawings show the same ancestry.]{color=#8D281E}

=== layout: two-panels

# Polytomy in Gene Trees

@left

![Original annotated polytomy diagram](assets/lect11b_restored/image_019.webp)

@right

- [*Polytomy*]{color=#784B04 underline=true}: a node with three or more branches.
- It can show insufficient information to resolve the branching order.
- Do not assume a resolved pair within that group.

@citation

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polytomy

=== layout: two-panels

# Tree to Pairwise Distance

@left

![Original scaled four-sequence tree](assets/lect11b_restored/image_027.webp)

@right

- Follow the branches between tips.
- Numbers label branch lengths; add every branch along the path.
- What are the distances a-c, a-d, and a-b?

=== layout: two-panels

# Add the Branches Along the Path

@left

![Original scaled four-sequence tree](assets/lect11b_restored/image_027.webp)

@right

- a to c: 3.2 + 3.2 = 6.4
- a to d: 3.2 + 3.1 + 6.3 = 12.6
- a to b: 3.2 + 3.1 + 2.4 + 8.7 = 17.4

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Shared nodes tell you ancestry and which sequences are most closely related.
- Rotating branches preserves relationships.
- A polytomy leaves some branching relationships unresolved.
- On a scaled tree, add the branches along a path to find the pairwise distance.

